+**Scope-carried values on the interactive surface.** The interactive surface
+is the HTML document served for a browser to render. It is not the
+machine-queryable endpoints (RFC6-13/14) and not any plain-text or exported
+rendering, which are every output that is not that document. On it only, a
+distinction other than `non-citable` / `presentation-artifact` whose value is
+the same for every unit under one enclosing element MAY be carried once on
+that element, marked machine-readably as a scope, in place of on each unit;
+this includes the evaluation identity RFC7-16 requires, and a scope-carried
+freshness stays visible on the page itself (RFC7-16). A unit under a scope
+then carries the value by expansion, under an inheritance rule the governing
+specification states once and every oracle restates independently. A scope is
+valid only if every unit under it would carry that same value on itself: it
+never overrides, replaces or fills in for a unit's own attribute, a unit that
+carries a different value of its own invalidates the scope, and an invalid
+scope is not emitted. A scope never carries a unit's identity, states what it
+carries as text on its own element (RFC7-34), and is expanded before any
+parity comparison. The machine-queryable endpoints and every plain-text or
+exported rendering carry every distinction on the unit itself, unchanged by
+this paragraph, and so does any copy, share or export function the surface
+offers: it is produced from the expanded model, never from the scoped markup.
+Only a reader's own manual selection of part of the page can separate a unit
+from its scope's text, which the scope states visibly on its own element.
+Non-citability is excluded because it must travel with a unit wherever it
+goes: the sub-clause below stands in full.
+
